{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '1,460p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp\n     2\t  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2): the WebSocket deposit\n     3\t   model over a socket that retains nothing.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `make-port` composes the local socket's protocol state, host-free: a\n     6\t   host adapter feeds every received datagram to `receive!` with its\n     7\t   raw bytes and the source address the socket saw. One CBOR value,\n     8\t   canonical profile, rides one datagram, at most `max-datagram` bytes,\n     9\t   fragment envelope included -- the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already\n    10\t   uses to clear common path MTUs. An encoded message over that budget\n    11\t   is split into fragment envelopes, channel-internal: neither the\n    12\t   mirror step nor the link (dao.stream.remote) ever sees one, only the\n    13\t   complete decoded message `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\n    14\t\n    15\t   Reassembly key, per 3.2: [attachment identity, source address,\n    16\t   direction, id]. For UDP the attachment identity a socket serves IS\n    17\t   the remote host:port it saw, so this port's own reassembly key\n    18\t   collapses the first two components into one address string; a\n    19\t   fragment's own carried id is unique only per asking side, so the\n    20\t   direction distinguishes a fragment split from an outgoing request\n    21\t   from one split from its answer, both landing at the same address\n    22\t   with the same id, when this side is both asker and mirror on one\n    23\t   channel. Bounded by two composition data, no clock:\n    24\t   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\n    25\t   oversize send and bounds a reassembly's accumulated chunk bytes;\n    26\t   and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n    27\t   partial message.\n    28\t\n    29\t   The deposit adapter shape: every reassembled datagram value is\n    30\t   deposited as one event, `{:dao.stream.udp/attachment id\n    31\t   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host h\n    32\t   :dao.stream.udp/port p} :dao.stream.udp/value v}`, onto the shared\n    33\t   traffic medium the composition wires -- one socket may serve several\n    34\t   attachments, one per remote address, so this medium is shared the\n    35\t   way a WebSocket connection's own traffic medium is not. `projection`\n    36\t   and `step!` are this channel's `ws-project`: driven per attachment\n    37\t   at the composition's cadence, they keep the events naming their own\n    38\t   attachment and append each value onto the channel ring that\n    39\t   attachment's reflections read.\n    40\t\n    41\t   `make-attacher` binds or reuses `:port` and answers a writer handle\n    42\t   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\n    43\t   and refusing oversize per the above; `send-to!` is the same write\n    44\t   path to an explicit destination from an already-bound port, for hole\n    45\t   punching (section 4), outside any attach!'d descriptor. Replies\n    46\t   always go to a datagram's own source address, never a payload's\n    47\t   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\n    48\t   read, and every deposited event carries the address the socket\n    49\t   itself saw it arrive from.\"\n    50\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    51\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor])\n    52\t  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(def max-datagram\n    59\t  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n    60\t   1200-byte figure dao.jing.dht.node already uses to clear common path\n    61\t   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\n    62\t  1200)\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(def default-max-message-bytes\n    66\t  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n    67\t  (* 64 1024))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(def default-max-partial-messages\n    71\t  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\n    72\t   on outstanding partial messages, chosen only to keep reassembly\n    73\t   memory finite without a clock.\"\n    74\t  64)\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn descriptor?\n    78\t  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity id\n    79\t     :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p} (3.2).\"\n    80\t  [x]\n    81\t  (and (map? x)\n    82\t       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n    83\t       (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n    84\t       (string? (:dao.stream.udp/host x))\n    85\t       (integer? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))\n    86\t       (pos? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn address-key\n    90\t  \"The attachment identity a socket serves for one remote address: the\n    91\t   host:port it saw, as a string, so it can key a map.\"\n    92\t  [host port]\n    93\t  (str host \":\" port))\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t;; =============================================================================\n    97\t;; Portable host byte arrays\n    98\t;; =============================================================================\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn- blen\n   101\t  [bs]\n   102\t  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n   103\t     :default (alength bs)))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- balloc\n   107\t  [n]\n   108\t  #?(:cljd (Uint8List. n)\n   109\t     :clj (byte-array n)\n   110\t     :cljs (js/Uint8Array. n)))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- bslice\n   114\t  [bs start end]\n   115\t  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (.sublist ^Uint8List bs start end))\n   116\t     :clj (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange ^bytes bs (int start) (int end))\n   117\t     :cljs (.slice bs start end)))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- bconcat\n   121\t  [chunks]\n   122\t  (let [total (reduce + (map blen chunks))\n   123\t        out (balloc total)]\n   124\t    (reduce (fn [off c]\n   125\t              (let [n (blen c)]\n   126\t                #?(:cljd (.setRange ^Uint8List out off (+ off n) c)\n   127\t                   :clj (System/arraycopy c 0 out off n)\n   128\t                   :cljs (.set out c off))\n   129\t                (+ off n)))\n   130\t            0 chunks)\n   131\t    out))\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\t(defn- ceil-div\n   135\t  [a b]\n   136\t  (quot (+ a (dec b)) b))\n   137\t\n   138\t\n   139\t;; =============================================================================\n   140\t;; Fragmentation (write side)\n   141\t;; =============================================================================\n   142\t\n   143\t(defn- direction-of\n   144\t  \"A request carries :dao.stream.remote/op; an answer never does.\"\n   145\t  [v]\n   146\t  (if (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/op)\n   147\t    :dao.stream.udp/request\n   148\t    :dao.stream.udp/answer))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t(defn- fragment-envelope\n   152\t  [id part parts direction chunk]\n   153\t  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n   154\t   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n   155\t   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n   156\t   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n   157\t   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn- fits?\n   161\t  [id part parts direction chunk-len]\n   162\t  (<= (blen (cbor/encode\n   163\t              (fragment-envelope id part parts direction\n   164\t                                 (balloc chunk-len))))\n   165\t      max-datagram))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t(defn- chunk-size\n   169\t  \"The largest chunk length whose fragment envelope, at the highest\n   170\t   part index (the widest of the small integers), still fits the\n   171\t   datagram budget; backs off from an optimistic start.\"\n   172\t  [id parts direction]\n   173\t  (loop [size (- max-datagram 16)]\n   174\t    (when (pos? size)\n   175\t      (if (fits? id (dec parts) parts direction size)\n   176\t        size\n   177\t        (recur (dec size))))))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defn- fragments\n   181\t  \"Split encoded `payload` for message `id`, direction `direction`,\n   182\t   into fragment envelopes that each fit the datagram budget.\"\n   183\t  [payload id direction]\n   184\t  (let [total (blen payload)\n   185\t        parts (max 1 (ceil-div total (- max-datagram 16)))\n   186\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction)\n   187\t                 (throw (ex-info\n   188\t                          \"dao.stream.udp: no chunk size fits the budget\"\n   189\t                          {:id id :parts parts})))\n   190\t        parts (max parts (ceil-div total size))\n   191\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction) size)]\n   192\t    (for [i (range parts)\n   193\t          :let [start (* i size)\n   194\t                end (min total (+ start size))]]\n   195\t      (fragment-envelope id i parts direction (bslice payload start end)))))\n   196\t\n   197\t\n   198\t;; =============================================================================\n   199\t;; The port: shared local-socket protocol state\n   200\t;; =============================================================================\n   201\t\n   202\t(defn make-port\n   203\t  \"Compose one local socket's protocol state. `:send!` is the host\n   204\t   seam, `(send! host port bytes) -> nil`, called once per outbound\n   205\t   datagram; it must not wait or throw for an ordinary transport\n   206\t   failure. `:traffic` is the shared deposit target the composition\n   207\t   wires -- a writer every reassembled datagram's event is appended to\n   208\t   (`dao.stream/writer?`). `:max-message-bytes` and\n   209\t   `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n   210\t   above).\"\n   211\t  [{:keys [send! traffic max-message-bytes max-partial-messages]}]\n   212\t  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n   213\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n   214\t                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n   215\t  (atom {:send! send!\n   216\t         :traffic traffic\n   217\t         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n   218\t                                default-max-message-bytes)\n   219\t         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n   220\t                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n   221\t         :partial {}\n   222\t         :partial-order []}))\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- send-datagram!\n   226\t  [port host dest-port bytes]\n   227\t  ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes))\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn send-value!\n   231\t  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n   232\t   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n   233\t   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n   234\t   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n   235\t   partially sent.\"\n   236\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   237\t  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n   238\t        n (blen payload)]\n   239\t    (cond\n   240\t      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n   241\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   242\t       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   243\t\n   244\t      (<= n max-datagram)\n   245\t      (do (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n   246\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\n   247\t\n   248\t      :else\n   249\t      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n   250\t            direction (direction-of v)]\n   251\t        (doseq [frag (fragments payload id direction)]\n   252\t          (send-datagram! port host dest-port (cbor/encode frag)))\n   253\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\t(defn send-to!\n   257\t  \"The explicit-destination write path from an already-bound port,\n   258\t   outside any attach!'d descriptor -- hole punching depends on it\n   259\t   (3.2, section 4).\"\n   260\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   261\t  (send-value! port host dest-port v))\n   262\t\n   263\t\n   264\t;; =============================================================================\n   265\t;; Reassembly (read side)\n   266\t;; =============================================================================\n   267\t\n   268\t(defn- fragment-envelope?\n   269\t  [v]\n   270\t  (and (map? v) (contains? v :dao.stream.udp/part)))\n   271\t\n   272\t\n   273\t(defn- well-formed-fragment?\n   274\t  \"A fragment envelope's own wire fields, checked before any reassembly\n   275\t   state can change: :part and :parts are integers with 0 <= :part <\n   276\t   :parts, 1 <= :parts, :direction one of the two the write side\n   277\t   mints, and :bytes a host byte payload. A datagram failing this is\n   278\t   best-effort wire input, dropped below the mirror and the link like\n   279\t   any other malformed one -- nothing here may throw during\n   280\t   reassembly, whatever a peer put on the wire.\"\n   281\t  [v]\n   282\t  (let [{:dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} v]\n   283\t    (and (integer? part)\n   284\t         (integer? parts)\n   285\t         (pos? parts)\n   286\t         (<= 0 part)\n   287\t         (< part parts)\n   288\t         (#{:dao.stream.udp/request :dao.stream.udp/answer} direction)\n   289\t         (cbor/byte-payload? bytes))))\n   290\t\n   291\t\n   292\t(defn- forget-partial!\n   293\t  \"Drop the partial message keyed `k` from the port's reassembly\n   294\t   state, its order entry with it.\"\n   295\t  [port k]\n   296\t  (swap! port (fn [s]\n   297\t                (-> s\n   298\t                    (update :partial dissoc k)\n   299\t                    (update :partial-order\n   300\t                            (fn [order] (vec (remove #{k} order))))))))\n   301\t\n   302\t\n   303\t(defn- evict-oldest!\n   304\t  [port]\n   305\t  (when-some [k (first (:partial-order @port))]\n   306\t    (forget-partial! port k)))\n   307\t\n   308\t\n   309\t(defn- absorb-fragment!\n   310\t  \"Fold one well-formed fragment envelope into the port's reassembly\n   311\t   state, keyed by [attachment direction id]; returns the complete\n   312\t   encoded payload once every part has arrived, else nil. The\n   313\t   max-message-bytes bound is the accumulated chunk bytes themselves,\n   314\t   as they arrive: a message whose parts add up past the bound is\n   315\t   dropped the moment they say so, and a completed payload is\n   316\t   delivered only if its own actual length is within the bound -- no\n   317\t   declared-parts arithmetic stands in for the real bytes, so a valid\n   318\t   message near the limit delivers. A duplicate part is ignored, and\n   319\t   a fragment disagreeing with the parts count a partial already\n   320\t   holds is dropped, so the state held is never torn.\"\n   321\t  [port attachment frag]\n   322\t  (let [{:dao.stream.remote/keys [id]\n   323\t         :dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} frag\n   324\t        k [attachment direction id]\n   325\t        bound (:max-message-bytes @port)]\n   326\t    (when-not (contains? (:partial @port) k)\n   327\t      (swap! port (fn [s]\n   328\t                    (-> s\n   329\t                        (assoc-in [:partial k]\n   330\t                                  {:parts {} :parts-count parts\n   331\t                                   :bytes-total 0})\n   332\t                        (update :partial-order conj k))))\n   333\t      (when (> (count (:partial @port))\n   334\t               (:max-partial-messages @port))\n   335\t        (evict-oldest! port)))\n   336\t    (when-some [held (get (:partial @port) k)]\n   337\t      (when (and (= parts (:parts-count held))\n   338\t                 (not (contains? (:parts held) part)))\n   339\t        (let [total (+ (:bytes-total held) (blen bytes))]\n   340\t          (if (> total bound)\n   341\t            ;; even completed, this message is over the bound: its\n   342\t            ;; partial state goes now, it can never deliver\n   343\t            (forget-partial! port k)\n   344\t            (do (swap! port (fn [s]\n   345\t                              (-> s\n   346\t                                  (assoc-in [:partial k :parts part] bytes)\n   347\t                                  (assoc-in [:partial k :bytes-total] total))))\n   348\t                (let [held (get (:partial @port) k)]\n   349\t                  (when (= (count (:parts held)) (:parts-count held))\n   350\t                    (forget-partial! port k)\n   351\t                    (let [payload (bconcat (mapv (:parts held)\n   352\t                                                 (range (:parts-count held))))]\n   353\t                      (when (<= (blen payload) bound)\n   354\t                        payload)))))))))))\n   355\t\n   356\t\n   357\t(defn- deposit!\n   358\t  [port attachment host src-port v]\n   359\t  (stream/append! (:traffic @port)\n   360\t                  {:dao.stream.udp/attachment attachment\n   361\t                   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host host\n   362\t                                           :dao.stream.udp/port src-port}\n   363\t                   :dao.stream.udp/value v}))\n   364\t\n   365\t\n   366\t(defn receive!\n   367\t  \"Adapter entry: one raw datagram arrived from `host`:`src-port` on\n   368\t   `port`'s socket, as `bytes`. Malformed CBOR is dropped as a\n   369\t   diagnostic below the mirror and the link, per dao.stream.remote.md\n   370\t   2.1, and so is a fragment envelope whose own fields are malformed\n   371\t   -- checked before any partial state can change. A plain value\n   372\t   deposits at once; a fragment envelope folds into reassembly and\n   373\t   deposits only once complete -- loss of any part is loss of the\n   374\t   whole message, recovered by the link's resend rule, not by this\n   375\t   port. Replies to what this call learns go to `host`:`src-port`,\n   376\t   this datagram's own source, never an address a payload might\n   377\t   claim.\"\n   378\t  [port host src-port bytes]\n   379\t  (when-some [v (try (cbor/decode bytes) (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   380\t                                                   :clj Throwable\n   381\t                                                   :cljs :default) _\n   382\t                                           nil))]\n   383\t    (let [attachment (address-key host src-port)]\n   384\t      (if (fragment-envelope? v)\n   385\t        (when (well-formed-fragment? v)\n   386\t          (when-some [payload (absorb-fragment! port attachment v)]\n   387\t            (when-some [whole (try (cbor/decode payload)\n   388\t                                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable\n   389\t                                             :cljs :default) _\n   390\t                                     nil))]\n   391\t              (deposit! port attachment host src-port whole))))\n   392\t        (deposit! port attachment host src-port v)))))\n   393\t\n   394\t\n   395\t;; =============================================================================\n   396\t;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment (this channel's\n   397\t;; ws-project)\n   398\t;; =============================================================================\n   399\t\n   400\t(defn projection\n   401\t  \"Compose one projection over the attachment identity `:attachment`\n   402\t   (an `address-key`), the shared traffic reader `:traffic` this port\n   403\t   deposits onto, the already-minted reading cursor `:cursor`, and the\n   404\t   channel ring `:ring` this attachment's reflections and mirror steps\n   405\t   read. Owns the cursor from here: each `step!` advances it.\"\n   406\t  [{:keys [attachment traffic cursor ring]}]\n   407\t  (when-not (and (string? attachment)\n   408\t                 (stream/reader? traffic)\n   409\t                 (some? cursor)\n   410\t                 (stream/reader? ring)\n   411\t                 (stream/writer? ring))\n   412\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp projection composition\"\n   413\t                    {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic\n   414\t                     :cursor cursor :ring ring})))\n   415\t  (atom {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic :cursor cursor\n   416\t         :ring ring}))\n   417\t\n   418\t\n   419\t(defn step!\n   420\t  \"Drive the projection once, at the composition's cadence: read the\n   421\t   traffic medium to blocked, keeping this attachment's own events and\n   422\t   dropping every other attachment's, appending each kept value onto\n   423\t   the channel ring. A gap adopts the medium's own recovery cursor.\n   424\t   Returns the projection.\"\n   425\t  [project]\n   426\t  (loop []\n   427\t    (let [s @project\n   428\t          r (stream/next (:traffic s) (:cursor s))]\n   429\t      (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   430\t        :dao.stream/ok\n   431\t        (let [event (:dao.stream/value r)]\n   432\t          (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   433\t          (when (= (:attachment s) (:dao.stream.udp/attachment event))\n   434\t            (stream/append! (:ring s) (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))\n   435\t          (recur))\n   436\t\n   437\t        :dao.stream/gap\n   438\t        (do (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   439\t            (recur))\n   440\t\n   441\t        project))))\n   442\t\n   443\t\n   444\t(defn reading-cursor\n   445\t  [project]\n   446\t  (:cursor @project))\n   447\t\n   448\t\n   449\t;; =============================================================================\n   450\t;; The writer handle and attacher\n   451\t;; =============================================================================\n   452\t\n   453\t(deftype UdpHandle\n   454\t  [port host dest-port descriptor]\n   455\t\n   456\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   457\t\n   458\t  (descriptor\n   459\t    [_]\n   460\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
