{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '65,100p;350,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"    65\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    66\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    67\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    68\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    69\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    70\t  peer (3.3).\n    71\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    72\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    73\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    74\t  state).\n    75\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    83\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    84\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    85\tend.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    91\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    92\t\n    93\t```clojure\n    94\t;; request\n    95\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    96\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor |\n    97\t                                       ; :dao.stream/cursor |\n    98\t                                       ; :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
